# Extraction Planning Phase 2 — bidirectional verification

Created under #130, not during the original arc. Phase 2 had **no** verification
log: its completion report records TDD red/green transitions but no
break/fail/restore probes, which is why the retroactive r3 review could not
confirm that the purity refactor's guards were load-bearing. This log covers the
#130 fix-forward for #124.

Tree: `develop` at `985ed1c` plus the uncommitted #130 fix-forward.

## Method notes

- **Every mutation must compile.** A mutant that fails `tsc -b` produces a
  `ReferenceError` cascade at runtime, which fails many more tests than the real
  behavioural difference and looks stronger while proving nothing. This trap fired
  once in this very fold — see probe 11 in `../phase-1/r2-verification.log`.
- **`tsc -b` exit status is captured directly**, never through a pipe: `$?` after a
  pipeline reports the last command in the pipe, not the compiler.
- Probe A's counts are for `src/ui/extraction-plan.test.ts` (29 cases after the
  fold adds 3 rows); probe B's are for `src/ui/GraphCanvas.dom.test.tsx` (10).

## Probe A — the purity precedence chain

Target: the `pure → normal → impure` guard chain in `src/ui/extraction-plan.ts`
(`highestPresentOutput`). Fixtures give `perExtractor` 240, so the arms yield
pure 480 → capacity `480`, normal 240 → `270`, impure 120 → `120`.

The retroactive r3 review found the chain's ordering unpinned by the pre-existing
`it.each` rows, each of which sets exactly one field -- with one field set, every
guard permutation picks the same arm, so none of them can detect a swap. (Read the
matrix below before generalising that: pure's precedence turned out to be pinned
elsewhere. The genuinely unpinned pair was normal-vs-impure.) Three mixed rows were
added, then each of the three pairwise swaps was applied separately and the full
kill matrix measured rather than assumed:

| Mutation (swap adjacent pair) | `tsc -b` | Failing rows | Focused count |
|---|---|---|---|
| pure ↔ normal | exit 0 | `{0,1,1}` **plus a pre-existing case** | 2 failed / 27 passed (29) |
| normal ↔ impure | exit 0 | `{1,1,0}` **only** | 1 failed / 28 passed (29) |
| pure ↔ impure (non-adjacent) | exit 0 | `{0,1,1}`, `{1,0,1}`, `{1,1,0}` | 4 failed / 25 passed (29) |

Representative RED, the normal↔impure swap:

```text
FAIL  src/ui/extraction-plan.test.ts > deriveExtractionPlan > uses the highest nonzero purity output for transport: { impure: '1', normal: '1', pure: '0' }
AssertionError: expected '120' to be '270' // Object.is equality
Tests  1 failed | 28 passed (29)
```

**What the matrix establishes, including against the fix-forward's own first
framing.** The normal↔impure swap is killed by exactly ONE test — the new
`{impure:1, normal:1, pure:0}` row. That was the only guard pair unpinned anywhere
in the repo, and that row is its sole killer, so it must not be trimmed as
redundant. The pure↔normal swap is killed by **two** tests, which is direct
evidence that pure's precedence was already covered before these rows existed (by
the clock-250 `{1,1,1}` case expecting 1200). The `{1,0,1}` row adds no unique
kill. An earlier version of the test comment claimed no adjacent pair was pinned;
that over-stated the gap and is corrected in the test file.

Restore and green:

```text
Tests  29 passed (29)
```

## Probe B — purity input DOM order

Target: the `purityFields` tuple in `src/ui/GraphCanvas.tsx`, from which the three
Impure/Normal/Pure inputs are rendered. Before the fix-forward, order had no
automated pin at all: every assertion selected inputs by aria-label, and the two
positional reads in the checked-in browser gate (`'0/5/0'` and `'1/1/1'`) are
symmetric across positions 1 and 3, so an impure↔pure reversal passed the entire
suite, `npm run check`, and every browser row.

Break — reverse the tuple to `pure, normal, impure`:

```text
npx tsc -b   → tsc_exit=0
```

RED:

```text
FAIL  src/ui/GraphCanvas.dom.test.tsx > RawFeedNode > enables, edits, preserves, and disables a solid node mix
AssertionError: expected [ 'Pure nodes', 'Normal nodes', …(1) ] to deeply equal [ Array(3) ]
Tests  1 failed | 9 passed (10)
```

Restore and green:

```text
Tests  39 passed (39)   (extraction-plan.test.ts + GraphCanvas.dom.test.tsx)
```

The assertion reads `.extraction-purity-fields input` in document order, and that
container holds exactly the three mapped inputs — the enable toggle sits outside
it — so a wrong order cannot pass.

